    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "That makes sense. Since blood meal breaks down faster, is there a specific way I should apply it to make sure it doesn't just wash away in the rain? Also, are there any other organic amendments you'd suggest for keeping the soil life active while I'm doing this?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "To prevent the valuable nutrients from blood meal from being washed away, apply it directly to the soil surface instead of mixing it into the soil. This way, it has less chance of being washed away by rain. Additionally, consider incorporating mulching with wood chips or bark to help reduce runoff and moisture evaporation.\n\n\nFor enhancing soil life organically, you can plant cover crops like clover or rye during the off-season to fix nitrogen and improve soil structure. You can also add aged composted manure to reintroduce beneficial microorganisms. Both practices will keep your soil healthy and active while you apply the blood meal. Sometimes it's also helpful to sprinkle poultry manure, which not only fertilizes the plants but also brings in beneficial nitrogen. Remember to ensure it's well decomposed before application to avoid any pathogen risks.",
